  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1239 PM CDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nThis Afternoon Through Friday: Beautiful Late August Weather!\n\nA center of low pressure is currently spinning over Lake Superior \nearly this afternoon and moisture continues to wrap around this \nfeature. The wrap around moisture is moving over the area at this \ntime, which is resulting in cloud cover increasing through the \nremainder of the day. Just enough lift may be present to generate \nisolated, light rain showers this afternoon. Rain totals will likely \nnot amount to much and any showers that develop will not remain over \nany one location for very long. Rain chances for the rest of the day \nare only upwards of 20 percent and mainly for the counties in \nWisconsin. The cloud cover along with the cooler airmass behind the \nfrontal passage from last night should lead to slightly cooler highs \nthis afternoon when compared to yesterday.\n\nThe upper level flow will become more northwesterly over the next \nfew days as the main axis of the H500 ridge slides east from over \nthe Rockies to over the western Great Plains. This pattern is not \nvery favorable for rain chances, so the forecast is dry on Thursday \nand during the day on Friday. The combination of light winds, mostly \nclear skies, and increasing low level temperatures will lead to \nsurface temperatures rising into the lower 80s for most by Friday \nafternoon. \n\nThis Weekend into Next Week: Rain and Storm Chances Return\n\nTroughing across eastern Canada with another trough digging south \nacross western Canada will begin to suppress the high pressure \nsitting across the middle part of the CONUS this weekend. As this \noccurs, mid to low level winds become more westerly to southwesterly \nwhich will bring positive theta-e advection to the region. \nShortwaves embedded in the flow should traverse over the Upper \nMidwest this weekend and showers/storms should be able to form as a \nresult. Timing and strength of the disturbances is variable in \nforecast guidance, so coverage of any rain and when the best chances \nwill be are uncertain for now. There may be a low potential for \nstorms to become strong to severe, but that will be highly dependent \nupon where the main axis of stronger instability sets up. The \nrain potential may continue into next week as shortwave troughs \ncontinue to drift over the region. Afternoon high temperatures \nwill be highly contingent on cloud cover and the potential \nprecipitation, but due to the continued warming of the low \nlevels, highs could reach the mid to upper 80s early next week.",
